WEBVTT

00:00:00.705 --> 00:00:01.422
A document.

00:00:01.442 --> 00:00:02.298
A road.

00:00:02.318 --> 00:00:03.048
A pitch.

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:04.248
A brick wall.

00:00:04.268 --> 00:00:06.922
Every photo of a flat thing is the same plane,

00:00:06.942 --> 00:00:10.555
seen through one three by three matrix: a
homography.

00:00:10.805 --> 00:00:12.810
Here's how it works on a square grid.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.398
First, lift each point: x, y becomes x, y,
one.

00:00:17.418 --> 00:00:18.798
Then multiply by H.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.285
Every point gets a third number, w.

00:00:21.305 --> 00:00:22.585
Now divide by w.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:25.685
Where w is big, points slide inward,

00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:28.855
and the square becomes a quadrilateral in
perspective.

00:00:28.985 --> 00:00:33.777
Follow the centre: one half, one half becomes
one, one, one and a half.

00:00:33.798 --> 00:00:36.677
Divided, it lands at two thirds, two thirds,

00:00:36.698 --> 00:00:40.135
where the diagonals cross, not at the average
of the corners.

00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:43.828
Lines that were parallel now meet, at a
vanishing point.

00:00:43.848 --> 00:00:48.378
Tilt the plane the other way too, and the
other family meets at a second point.

00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:50.910
The line through both is the horizon.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.370
So how many point pairs pin down H?

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.058
It has nine numbers, but scaling them all
changes nothing, so eight unknowns.

00:00:58.078 --> 00:01:00.082
Each pair gives two equations.

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:01.107
How many pairs?

00:01:01.128 --> 00:01:02.715
Pause and decide.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.428
Four pairs, eight equations.

00:01:07.448 --> 00:01:12.435
Drag the four corners, and H is solved again
at once; every other point follows.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.070
Now a real photo: a brick wall, seen at an
angle.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.415
Put the four corners on mortar lines.

00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:22.672
To make the flat view, work backwards: for
each output pixel,

00:01:22.692 --> 00:01:25.310
H inverse says where it came from in the
photo,

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.848
and we blend the four nearest pixels.

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:29.955
The bricks come out rectangular.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.930
The same matrix stitches panoramas, flattens
scanned documents,

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.305
tracks players on a pitch, and warps training
images.

00:01:37.325 --> 00:01:40.550
And spatial transformer networks learn their
own H.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.658
So remember three things.

00:01:42.678 --> 00:01:44.958
Lift points to x, y, one.

00:01:44.977 --> 00:01:47.482
Multiply by H, then divide by w.

00:01:47.502 --> 00:01:49.715
And four point pairs fix H.

